Contributing to Keira Kernel
============================

Welcome to the Keira Kernel project! Keira is an open-source, freestanding
Ring 0 monolithic kernel built from first principles in safe Rust, Assembly
and C, targeting both 64-bit (x86_64) and 32-bit (i686) architectures.

We welcome technical contributions including bug fixes, hardware device
drivers, architecture enhancements, optimization passes and documentation
improvements.

Quick Contributor Reference
---------------------------

Before proposing or submitting changes, please review our comprehensive
technical documentation in the `docs/process/` tree:

* Contributor Guide: docs/process/contributing.rst
* Submitting Patches & PRs: docs/process/submitting-patches.rst
* Coding Style & Invariants: docs/process/coding-style.rst
* Development Workflow: docs/process/development-workflow.rst
* Build System Reference: docs/process/build-system.rst
* Verification & Testing: docs/process/verification-and-testing/index.rst
* Subsystem Maintainers: MAINTAINERS
* Technical Credits: CREDITS

Architectural Invariants & Non-Negotiables
------------------------------------------

1. Monolithic Kernel Boundary: Keira is an autonomous Ring 0 monolithic
   kernel. Ring 3 userland processes run in strict isolation. Faults in
   userspace must be trapped and isolated; they must never panic Ring 0.
2. Dual-Architecture Parity: All changes must compile and pass testing on
   both x86_64 and i686 architectures with 0 errors and 0 warnings.
3. Zero Warnings Policy: All 12 kernel crates, assembly stubs, userland C
   code and Sphinx documentation must compile cleanly without warnings.
4. SPDX Licensing: Every source file must contain a machine-readable
   SPDX-License-Identifier tag (GPL-2.0-only for kernel source and docs).
5. Clean Style: Zero Oxford commas in prose and comments. Zero double
   whitespaces. No decorative comment dividers (such as repeated ==== or ----)
   in source code comments.

Local Verification Sequence
---------------------------

Execute the following quality gates before opening a pull request:

    # 1. Format code across Rust and C trees
    make format

    # 2. Run static analysis on userland C code and documentation
    make lint

    # 3. Verify documentation compliance with style guide
    make docs-lint

    # 4. Run host unit tests across all 12 kernel crates
    make test-unit

    # 5. Build and verify userland diagnostic test binaries
    make user-test ARCH=x86_64
    make user-test ARCH=i686

    # 6. Build kernel binaries and ISO images for all architectures
    make full

    # 7. Run automated headless smoke boot tests
    make test-all

Submitting Your Work
--------------------

* Organize changes into small, atomic and bisectable commits following
  conventional commit syntax: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`.
* Include a `Signed-off-by` trailer in each commit message.
* Submit pull requests against the `main` branch.
